.TH GH-CUSTOM-PROPERTIES 1 "16 September 2026" "gh-custom-properties 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
gh-custom-properties \- view, add, edit, and delete GitHub custom repo properties
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gh-custom-properties [flags] [owner/repo | repo-url]
.B gh-custom-properties --file repos.txt [flags]
.B gh-custom-properties --list [flags] owner/repo
.B gh-custom-properties --set name=value [--set name=value ...] [flags] owner/repo
.SH DESCRIPTION
If no repo argument or --file is given, the TUI prompts for a repo to load.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B -f, --file <path>
batch mode: file listing one repo (owner/repo or URL) per line
.TP
.B --list
non-interactive: print the repo's custom properties as name=value lines and exit
.TP
.B --set <name=value>
non-interactive: set a property on the repo; repeat for multiple. Multi-select values are comma-separated; an empty value clears the property
.TP
.B -t, --token <token>
GitHub token, overriding GITHUB_TOKEN / `gh auth token`
.TP
.B --backup-dir <path>
directory to write timestamped backups to (default $HOME/.gh-custom-properties/backups)
.TP
.B -h, -H, -?, --help, --HELP
show help
.TP
.B -v, --version
show version
.SH AUTHENTICATION
A GitHub token is required, resolved in this order:
  1. --token
  2. GITHUB_TOKEN environment variable
  3. `gh auth token` (if the gh CLI is installed and logged in)
.PP
The token needs permission to read and write custom properties on the
target repos, and to read the org's custom-property schema.
.SH REPO LIST FILE FORMAT (--FILE)
One repo per line, as "owner/repo" or a github.com URL. Blank lines and
lines starting with '#' are ignored.
.SH NON-INTERACTIVE MODE (--LIST, --SET)
Both require a repo argument and cannot be combined with --file. With
both, --set is applied first and the updated properties are listed.
Values are validated against the org's property schema when readable
(unknown names, disallowed select values, non-boolean true_false values,
and clearing required properties are rejected before anything is written).
--list output goes to stdout; --set progress and the backup path go to stderr.
.PP
Example:
  gh-custom-properties --set team=platform --set tags=a,b --list my-org/my-repo
.SH BACKUPS
Before any change is applied, a timestamped JSON snapshot of the prior
property values is written to the backup directory.
